# PART 7 — MOTION & INTERACTION SYSTEM

Create one unified motion specification.

## 7.1 Ambient motion

Preserve the living Hero behavior approved in BR-T020.

For new scenes, propose subtle motion only where it improves the experience.

Examples:

- Gentle environmental movement.
- Independent foreground elements.
- Slow depth changes.
- Small character movements.
- Illustrated object reactions.

Avoid making every section equally animated.

## 7.2 User-triggered animation

Prioritize animations connected to actions:

- Hover/focus on a destination.
- Card inspection.
- Selecting a game.
- Page transitions.
- Entering an illustrated scene.
- Revealing educational information.
- Later: drawing/flipping a digital card.

Hover-only functionality is prohibited.

Touch and keyboard users must have equivalent access to important behavior.

## 7.3 Motion implementation

Specify appropriate techniques:

- CSS transitions/keyframes.
- React state.
- IntersectionObserver.
- requestAnimationFrame when necessary.
- Subtle scroll parallax.
- Motion libraries only if justified.

Avoid heavy WebGL/3D dependencies without a demonstrated need.

Respect reduced-motion settings.

Do not introduce aggressive flashing or scroll hijacking.

## 7.4 Accessibility decision record

The current Hero has an approved product decision to run continuous ambient animation without an in-page pause control.

This is a known WCAG 2.2.2 limitation.

Preserve the documented decision but do not incorrectly claim full WCAG 2.2 AA compliance.

For newly designed scenes, explicitly assess movement-related accessibility rather than automatically extending this exception to the whole site.

